 by this growing inequality  between education  it seems that there's been an arms race  in the private sector for years right  somebody builds a theater somebody else  has to build an even grander sports  pitch the facilities race  in the private sector has been quite  extraordinary i think a lot of that's  been about attracting people from  overseas i don't think it bears any  relationship to what happens in the  state system here or or anywhere else  come to that and i also don't think it's  necessarily has a great deal to do with  the education i don't think it you don't  think it it entrenches inequality  between those children that can go to a  private school and those children that  can't it does entrench some undesirable  kinds of equality but if we look across  the world  state systems everywhere in developed  countries are operating with quite  similar amounts of funding english  schools are very much  in the normal range so the first job is  to make sure we use what we've got  really well and think in a very  structured way about curriculum and  teaching and make sure that children  have have the the very best experience  well look well we believe we can make we  make fantastic schools okay well let's  come on to your strategy you put out a  five-year strategy this week to try and  address the devastating impact on the  pandemic with a big focus on the early  years flagging that children  lagging there are children lagging in  communication and languages schools some  of the pretty skills some of the the  preschool kids  is that your your biggest area of  concern when you look across the piece  of early years to secondary education  we've got concerns right through  we've done a particularly large amount  of work on school age and  post post-16 over the last five years  in the light of covid we've seen  particular harm for the youngest  children and remember children who have  just turned two say have lived all their  lives in a pandemic they've seen adults  around them with face masks they've seen  far fewer people than usual it's not  surprising that for so many of them  their language and communication  development are well behind where they  would normally be that their social  skills are less developed so getting  them  back on track has got to be a really big  priority and that's that's why we've put  it right in the center of our new  strategy and as i understand it the  cabinet talked about this week  looking at  cutting child care costs because of the  cost of living crisis by loot diluting  the ratio of adults to kids in early  years education you know that you know  you can one adult at the moment i think  has three children  would you worry about that in the  context of what you're talking about  that actually they need more input is  that a concern to you  i think it's really important um to look  to look to look comparatively here  um i've seen oecd analysis  that says we've got the most generous  staffing ratios in the developed world  um for early education of course there's  a trade-off between between staffing and  cost it's very important that we protect  the quality of education and the quality  of care for children but but but i do  think it's right to look and make to  look to look at it to make to make sure  that we're not goal plating that we're  making that that we've got early  education um as affordable as it can be  as well as  quite really high quality so in  principle you're not opposed to the  loosening of those ratios in principle i  don't think ratios of themselves drive  quality we should look at quality  quality of education quality of care and  safeguarding first and foremost okay  one other thing i wanted to  ask you about in relation to  children's education is something that  the government social mobility  commissioner said yesterday she told a  group of mps that girls shy away from  studying physics because they don't like  hard maths what was your  response to that what do y