 woodhead or michael  wilshaw they were fellow teachers and  you aren't you were someone that was  from the city trained and worked in the  city has that been a barrier for you  with teachers  do you think that sometimes they think  you don't understand the pressures  they're under because you haven't walked  in their shoes  i'm sure that happens sometimes but i  think what people lose sight of  is that it was actually only until my  late 30s that i worked in the city and  then i changed career and i've worked in  education  um since  for the last 20 years  so i did a masters in education and then  i became part of the founding team at  what's now one of the biggest academy  chains so i have lived and breathed  education from the point of view of  somebody um running an academy chain  um really getting into the problems of  the most disadvantaged schools um the  chain i worked for um was particularly  targeted at children with the  the steepest path to climb we were  taking on the toughest schools  working out what curriculum what  teaching were needed working out what it  would take to to turn those schools  around and i was doing that for years  before i came into this job so i have  seen  all the things that can go wrong with  the school and all the things that can  go right with the school at very close  quarters and been working intimately  with the people who are directly  responsible in those schools and amanda  coming on to  working in schools and some of the  the cultural issues around schools  in the u.s there's  been a situation where schools and  curricula is becoming a battleground a  political battleground do you fear that  happening here you recently said that  schools have to tread a careful path  between being damned for being  intolerant or slammed for being woke  what do you mean by that  this country fortunately is is not  polarized at the level of that i think  we're seeing in the united united states  but nevertheless there are some very  very 